    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d185">

                <pb facs="0319" n="277" sameAs="frus:frus1863p1/pg277"/>

                <head>Mr. Seward to Mr.
                        Adams.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 579.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Washington,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1863-05-05"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >May</hi> 5, 1863.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> You will learn by the journals that General
                    Hooker has crossed the Rappahannock and taken a position at Chancellorsville,
                    beyond the enemy at Fredericksburg. As the importance of this movement is
                    apparent, there has been intense anxiety here for the development of its
                    anticipated results. I had hoped to be able to give you some light upon the
                    subject in this despatch. General Hooker, however, keeps his counsels closely,
                    and, as we must suppose, wisely.</p>
                <p>I hardly need refer you to the gratifying intelligence which comes from Louisiana
                    and the Mississippi. The campaign in those regions seems to be advancing
                    prosperously.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>WILLIAM H. SEWARD.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles Francis Adams</hi>, Esq., <hi
                            rendition="#italic">&amp;c., &amp;c., &amp;c.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
